SI-v2: A Scoped Confinement Architecture for Untrusted Agent Actions

Ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM

Date: 20 October 2026

Status discipline: [H] architectural hypothesis · [C] conceptually specified · [V] obligation stated, not discharged · [F] claim withdrawn or expected to fail · [S] not simulated, not implemented, not physically tested

Abstract

This monograph specifies SI-v2, a safety-invariance monitoring architecture for untrusted agent actions. The architecture separates proposal, verification, execution control, and recovery, and is positioned explicitly within the Runtime Assurance lineage (Simplex; ASTM F3269-21). No invention of that separation is claimed. The claimed delta is narrower: an uncertainty-envelope-bound certificate whose checker cost is bounded independently of the prover, a viability-grounded fallback built on a measurement-robust backup set, and a stratified policy-revision calculus whose side conditions are machine-checkable.

The document is theoretical. No implementation, simulation, formal proof, hardware test, or empirical measurement is reported. Every guarantee is expressed as a proof obligation with an explicit status. The central falsifiable claim is stated as a theorem schema: under declared model assumptions, if the true state lies in the propagated envelope and the monitor accepts an action, then the true successor lies in a control-invariant backup set K. That schema is decomposed into obligations, several of which are expected to fail on the second reference plant. The monograph therefore specifies a syntax for claiming safety, together with a precise account of what would falsify each claim—not a safety certificate.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Problem

An intelligent agent may propose arbitrary actions. Its ability to propose is not its authority to execute. The engineering question is not whether the agent is aligned, but whether a trusted boundary can confine the physical consequences of an unverified proposal to a set whose invariance has been established independently of the agent.

1.2 Positioning

The Proposal–Verification–Execution–Recovery separation is the Runtime Assurance pattern. ASTM F3269-21 standardizes RTA as an alternative to design-time assurance for complex, unassured functions: a monitor observes the inputs and outputs of the complex function and activates a verified recovery function when safe bounds are breached. The Simplex architecture is the canonical instantiation. SI-v2 adopts this lineage and claims no priority over it.

Two further literatures bound the claim. Proof-carrying agent actions (PCAA) require each governed action to carry a certificate that a planner-independent checker accepts before execution, bound to the exact invocation by a canonical cryptographic commitment. Measurement-robust control barrier functions (MR-CBF) provide the tightening that makes a barrier condition sound under bounded state-estimation error.

1.3 Claimed delta

SI-v2 claims novelty in exactly two places, and nowhere else.

1. The certificate is bound to an uncertainty envelope, not a point estimate. The accepted action must be robustly safe for every state in a propagated envelope \mathcal{X}_t, and the envelope's soundness is itself an obligation, not an assumption.
2. Policy revision is stratified into R1/R2/R3 with machine-checkable side conditions. Cryptographic quorum proves who approved a change; it does not prove why the change preserves safety. The calculus makes the safety argument of a revision a precondition for activation.

The fallback, watchdog, isolation, and refinement mechanisms are adaptations of established practice, specified here only to the extent required to make the two deltas executable and falsifiable.

1.4 Status discipline

Every statement in this monograph carries one of five labels. [H] denotes an architectural hypothesis. [C] denotes a concept specified but not formalized. [V] denotes an obligation stated but not discharged. [F] denotes a claim withdrawn or expected to fail under stated conditions. [S] denotes that no simulation, implementation, or physical test has been performed. A reader who finds an unlabeled safety claim has found an error.

