4. Uncertainty Envelope

4.1 Measurement-consistent set

X_t^{(0)} = \{\, x \in X : \| y_t - h(x) \| \le \epsilon(y_t) \,\}.

4.2 Propagation

The v0.3 draft propagated from X_t^{(0)}, discarding the prior envelope's information. The correct propagation starts from the prior envelope:

\mathcal{X}_{t+\Delta} \;=\; \mathrm{Red}_m\!\left( \mathrm{Reach}(\mathcal{X}_t,\, a_{\mathrm{held}},\, W,\, \Delta) \;\cap^{\sim}\; X_{t+\Delta}^{(0)} \right).

The operator \cap^{\sim} is a sound over-approximating intersection: the intersection of a zonotope with a strip is not a zonotope, so the monitor must name and prove an operator whose result contains the true intersection. The operator is an open implementation obligation [V]; it is not a settled design choice.

4.3 Order reduction

\mathrm{Red}_m reduces to at most m = 32 generators. The reduction replaces Z with Z_R \supseteq Z; the added width w(Z_R) - w(Z) is recorded as slack and carried into the envelope accounting. A reduction without a proved soundness bound is rejected.

4.4 Conservatism accounting, defined per direction

The v0.3 slack ratio \sigma_t = w(\mathcal{X}_t)/w(X_t^{(0)}) is undefined when X_t^{(0)} is unbounded, as with position-only sensing. The ratio is therefore defined per direction:

\sigma_t^{(i)} \;=\; \frac{\mathrm{width}_i(\mathcal{X}_t)}{\mathrm{width}_i(X_t^{(0)})}, \qquad i \in \{1,\ldots,n\},

with the convention that a direction with unbounded X_t^{(0)} is excluded from the diagnostic and reported separately. A median \sigma_t^{(i)} > 3.0 over a declared evaluation window indicates that the envelope method, not the agent, is the dominant cause of intervention. This is a design failure of the envelope method and is reported as such.

4.5 Invalidation

The envelope is invalid if X_t^{(0)} = \emptyset, if any fixed-point saturation flag is set, if the propagation horizon is exceeded, or if the disturbance-model version does not match the active registry entry. An invalid envelope does not reach the proof checker; the monitor enters mitigation.

