5. Robust Safety, Viability, and the Feasibility Gate

5.1 One-step robustness

\mathrm{RobustSafe}(a, \mathcal{X}, W) \iff \forall x \in \mathcal{X},\, \forall w \in W:\; F(x,a,w) \in S_{\mathrm{safe}}.

Necessary, insufficient. A one-step-safe action can leave the system in a state from which no future safe action exists.

5.2 The backup set and Obligation 0

Exact viability kernels are intractable in general. SI-v2 uses a backup-set under-approximation: a backup controller k_B is fixed, and K is the set from which the backup trajectory remains in S_{\mathrm{safe}} for the declared horizon and reaches a known invariant terminal set.

The v0.3 draft set K = \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon), which changes with \epsilon and therefore does not cover invariance across steps with varying error bounds. The corrected definition fixes K at the most conservative bound:

K \;:=\; \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_{\max}).

The invariance obligation is stated in its strong form, quantifying over every error bound below the budget:

Obligation 0 (K-invariance). [V]

> \forall x \in K,\; \forall e : \|e\| \le \epsilon_{\max},\; \forall w \in W:\; F(x, k_B(x+e), w) \in K.
> 

The K of §5.2 is a fixed set, not a function of the envelope. The phrase “viability kernel of the uncertainty envelope” used in earlier drafts is withdrawn. The envelope enters through the antecedent x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t and through the measurement-robust tightening of §7.

5.3 Feasibility gate: measurement-error budget

The v0.2 gate used a volume functional V_{\max}. The v0.3 draft replaced it with a hull-width product. Both are shape-insensitive: a long thin envelope can cross K with a small width product. The gate is therefore stated per constraint.

For the backup set \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon), compute offline

\epsilon_{\max}(x) = \sup\{\, \epsilon : \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) \text{ is invariant under } k_B \text{ and } \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) \subseteq S_{\mathrm{safe}} \,\}.

This budget is scalar and monotone in \epsilon. At runtime, if \|\epsilon(y_t)\| > \epsilon_{\max}(\hat{x}_t), the nominal guarantee is unavailable under current measurement quality. The monitor bypasses the proof checker and enters mitigation deterministically. If the budget holds, the monitor proceeds, and the per-constraint margins

\mu_i(\mathcal{X}_t) = b_i - \left( h_i^{\top} c + \| G^{\top} h_i \|_1 \right), \qquad i = 1,\ldots,\ell,

are checked non-negative. The budget and the margins answer different questions—is any certificate meaningful? and does this envelope satisfy this constraint?—and neither substitutes for the other.

