8. Monitor Architecture

The decision path is four layers, each with a declared trust boundary and resource bound.

Layer 0 — framing and parsing. no_std, fixed 16 KiB arena, canonical decoder with maximum depth 8, no dynamic allocation. The parser is checked with Kani for panic-freedom, bounds safety, and integer overflow over the declared domain. A fixed-layout binary frame is preferred over nested CBOR for the same reason: it is cheaper to verify, and the canonicalisation rules are simpler to state.

Layer 1 — freshness and policy consistency. q strictly increasing; p_{\mathrm{hash}} in the trusted allowlist; p_{\mathrm{id}} strictly monotonic; d > \mathrm{now} + \Delta_{\mathrm{WCET}}. Rollback is rejected before the proof checker runs.

Layer 2 — proof and viability checking. A time-boxed checker with a declared operation budget, on an isolated core or interrupt context. It verifies \rho and the boxed clauses of §6. The budget is stated as an operation count per mode, not an asymptotic class. A timeout is treated as rejection.

Layer 3 — decision and evidence. Every decision is hash-chained into an append-only log. The authorization token binds the action hash, q, an observation-commitment hash, and the expiry d. The observation commitment is a hash of the envelope-construction inputs, not merely the sequence number; otherwise a replayed token with a different envelope would pass.

Watchdog. A single heartbeat proves only that the interrupt handler runs. The architecture requires two independent mechanisms: a hardware watchdog on a separate MCU that monitors lease renewal, and a time-triggered partition in which the agent and monitor do not share a scheduler. If the monitor stalls, the watchdog activates the fallback directly, without routing through the stalled monitor.

Actuation channel. The authorization-to-actuation path is a hardware mailbox or an isolated message channel the agent cannot overwrite. Shared memory writable by the agent is not an execution channel.

---

9. Adversary Model

The agent is untrusted; its capabilities are bounded explicitly.

· Sensor influence is possible only with write access to the sensor bus. Observations lacking provenance are rejected.
· Timing attacks are closed by monotonic q and a deadline d.
· Flooding is bounded by Layer 0 size limits and Layer 1 sequence checks; the monitor's worst-case service rate is part of the WCET analysis.
· TOCTOU is closed structurally: the actuator re-verifies the token's binding to the action hash, q, the observation commitment, and d immediately before committing the action. If the state digest has changed beyond tolerance, the actuator refuses.

The agent is not assumed to have arbitrary write access to monitor memory or the policy store. If it does, no runtime monitor can provide the claimed guarantee; that is a platform isolation precondition, not a monitor property.

