12. Policy Revision Calculus

The R1/R2/R3 taxonomy is retained with machine-checkable side conditions.

R1 — restrictive refinement. The v0.3 draft required S_{\min} \subseteq S_{k+1} and a per-revision ratio \alpha. A per-revision ratio compounds: \alpha = 0.95 over fourteen revisions yields roughly 0.49. The condition is therefore stated against a fixed baseline,

w(S_{k+1}) \ge \beta \, w(S_{\mathrm{baseline}}),

together with trajectory preservation: every entry region retains at least one verified nominal trajectory of length T_{\mathrm{avail}}. The first condition prevents collapse; the second prevents erosion of usefulness while leaving a non-empty set of dead states. Both are checked in Lean over the abstract policy semantics, not inferred from the version number.

R2 — model correction. The replacement requires a new envelope-soundness proof, a documented correction argument, and re-discharge of all affected obligations. A proof of inconsistency of the old model does not identify a correct replacement; the replacement is a new model requiring its own soundness evidence.

R3 — governance or domain extension. The change requires an assurance-case delta identifying new hazards introduced by the expansion, a new model-validity assessment, and authorization under the governance policy. Cryptographic quorum establishes that authorized participants approved; the assurance-case delta establishes why the change is claimed safe. Neither substitutes for the other.

Rollback. The policy store is append-only and hash-chained; p_{\mathrm{id}} is strictly monotonic. A certificate referencing a non-monotonic p_{\mathrm{id}} is rejected at Layer 1.

