13. Refinement Pipeline

The Rust-to-Lean claim of v0.2 was too strong as written. The extraction toolchain has known boundaries: it requires monomorphised types, does not extract external-crate MIR, and has limited support for unsafe. A refinement claim that ignores these boundaries is not a refinement claim.

Three levels.

· L1 — extraction. Charon extracts rustc MIR into LLBC; Aeneas translates LLBC into a pure Lean 4 model. The extracted model contains no sorry. Files with extraction failures are tagged INCONCLUSIVE.
· L2 — differential and bounded checking. Sampled inputs are exercised by tests; Kani exhaustively checks the parser and checker for panic-freedom, overflow, and bounds over the declared word width. Kani results are bounded and are not presented as unbounded proofs.
· L3 — refinement theorem. [V]
  \forall \pi_t:\; \mathrm{ExtractedMonitor}(\pi_t) = \mathrm{Accept} \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathrm{AbstractContract}(\pi_t).

The Lean model and the Rust code share the fixed-point semantics of §3. The theorem therefore speaks about the compiled arithmetic, including saturation flags, rather than an idealised rational model.

CI gate. Any file tagged INCONCLUSIVE blocks policy activation. The gate is a semantic necessity, not a bureaucratic addition: the toolchain's design philosophy is to fail early rather than risk certifying unsupported constructs.

Trusted computing base inventory. [V] The TCB includes the extraction toolchain, the Lean kernel, the offline artifacts of §5, the held-control reporting channel, and the sensor-provenance source. A bug in the offline viability computation or the \epsilon_{\max} table invalidates the runtime guarantee as surely as a parser bug. The offline artifacts require independent certification—by a separate checker or a Lean proof—before the runtime monitor may rely on them.

