The following obligations are stated, not discharged. No safety case is asserted until all are machine-checked.

ID Obligation Status
O-1 Initial-state safety: X_0 \subseteq S_{\mathrm{safe}} [V]
O-2 Envelope soundness: the fixed-point zonotope with sound intersection and order reduction contains the true reachable set [V]
O-3 Arithmetic soundness: outward rounding and explicit error generator, including saturation semantics [V]
O-4 One-step robustness: accepted actions map \mathcal{X}_t into S_{\mathrm{safe}} for all w \in W [V]
O-5 Obligation 0: K-invariance under k_B for every \|e\| \le \epsilon_{\max}, under delay and hold [V], expected to fail on Plant 2 [F]
O-6 End-to-end confinement theorem schema [V], not a theorem until O-5 is discharged
O-7 Authorization soundness of the extracted monitor, not a restatement of Accept [V]
O-8 Policy integrity across R1/R2/R3, including baseline availability and rollback rejection [V]
O-9 Fallback correctness: when A_{\mathrm{safe}} \neq \emptyset, the selected fallback lies in A_{\mathrm{safe}}; when empty, mitigation is entered and no safety claim is made [V]
O-10 TCB inventory completeness, including offline artifacts [V]

End-to-end theorem schema. [V]

Assume O-2, O-3, O-5, and O-7. If the true state x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t and the monitor accepts a_t, then for every admitted w \in W the true successor satisfies x_{t+1} \in K \subseteq S_{\mathrm{safe}}, and by induction x_{t+k} \in S_{\mathrm{safe}} for all k \ge 0 along the backup trajectory.

The theorem is not “the system is safe.” It is: under the stated assumptions, the accepted action preserves membership in the backup set, and the backup set is invariant. The delay requires state augmentation with held controls, and the induction must account for the held-control sequence.

