15. Pre-Registered Falsification Protocol

The following criteria are fixed before any simulation. They are reported here as pre-registrations, not as results [S]. A single violation falsifies the corresponding claim.

ID Criterion Threshold
F1 Monitor accepts an action exiting K under an admitted disturbance any occurrence
F2 Monitor accepts a replayed q or a rolled-back p_{\mathrm{id}} any occurrence
F3 Decision path exceeds the measured high-water mark plus static bound high-water mark > bound
F4 Fallback infeasible where A_{\mathrm{safe}} \neq \emptyset any occurrence
F5 False-reject rate on nominal corpus > 5\%
F6 Median per-direction envelope slack > 3.0
F7 Monitor accepts a certificate where \|\epsilon(y)\| > \epsilon_{\max} any occurrence

Baselines. A no-RTA planner, a current-state monitor checking against \hat{x}_t rather than \mathcal{X}_t, and a Simplex baseline without the viability clause. The baselines isolate the contribution of envelope robustness and viability preservation.

Search method. A falsification criterion is only as strong as the search that failed to violate it. The protocol requires adversarial fuzzing of the framing layer and certificate fields, plus a search tool targeting F1 and F4, with coverage reported. “No violation observed” without a stated search method is not evidence.

WCET. A p99 measurement cannot falsify a WCET claim. The criterion is a measured high-water mark combined with a static bound, under a declared cache and memory-bus interference assumption. If the interference bound is not established on the target platform, the real-time claim is withdrawn.

Model mismatch. A separate campaign perturbs the plant outside W and measures degradation. No safety claim is made from this campaign; its purpose is to document the boundary of the model's validity.

