16. Reference Plants

Plant 1 — discrete-time double integrator with actuation delay. x = (p,v) \in \mathbb{R}^2, u \in \mathbb{R}, w \in W = [-\bar w, \bar w]^2, \Delta = 2. The successor is affine and the envelope is a zonotope. Mode L applies. This plant tests the envelope mechanics, the intersection operator, the order reduction, the per-constraint gate, and the availability of the budget.

Plant 2 — nonlinear pendulum (unicycle deferred). The pendulum is selected first because its Lipschitz constants are easy to bound. Mode N applies. This plant tests the measurement-robust tightening, the Farkas implication, and the registry-supplied constants. It is the plant on which the certificate is not redundant, because the monitor cannot recompute the successor set in closed form. Obligation 0 is expected to fail here; if it does, the end-to-end claim is withdrawn for this plant, and the monograph records the withdrawal.

---

17. Scope, Limitations, and Threats to Validity

This monograph specifies a syntax for claiming safety, not a safe system. The following are explicitly not claimed:

· That the envelope is sound for any plant beyond the two reference plants until O-2 and O-3 are discharged.
· That K is the exact viability kernel; it is a backup-set under-approximation, and its conservatism is reported.
· That the refinement proof has been completed or that no file is INCONCLUSIVE.
· That WCET has been measured on protected hardware with independent watchdogs and a bounded memory bus.
· That the fallback is feasible in all reachable states; Table 2 may weaken the claim to conditional.
· That the median slack is below the threshold on any plant not yet simulated.
· That the system prevents every unsafe physical action.

Three threats to validity are stated explicitly. Citation validity: the PCAA latency figure used in earlier drafts is withdrawn; the comparison to PCAA is structural, not quantitative, and the earlier specific figure is not used. Model validity: a proof about \mathcal{M} is not a proof about the plant; the model-mismatch campaign documents the boundary but does not close it. Toolchain validity: the refinement theorem is only as trustworthy as the Lean kernel, the extraction toolchain, and the compiler; these are in the TCB and require independent assurance.

---

18. Conclusion

The separation of proposal, authority, uncertainty, and execution is sound but not novel. The contribution of this monograph is to make the separation falsifiable: to bind the certificate to an uncertainty envelope whose soundness is an obligation, to ground the fallback in a control-invariant backup set whose invariance is an obligation under measurement error, and to stratify policy revision so that the safety argument of a change is a precondition for activation.

The central falsifiable research objective is: for the declared reference plants and model assumptions, demonstrate that the accepted-action set is a subset of the control-invariant backup set K, that the compiled monitor preserves this subset relation as established by Rust-to-Lean refinement over shared fixed-point semantics, and that the pre-registered falsification criteria are not violated under a stated adversarial search.

Until that objective is met and independently reviewed, SI-v2 remains a proposed architecture with a precise, auditable failure criterion. The value of the specification lies not in what it claims to guarantee, but in what it declares would count as a refutation. A safety architecture that cannot be falsified cannot be trusted; one that names its own failure conditions, and states which of them it expects to fail, can at least be audited.

Appendices

Status key. [P] paper proof, derived in this document, not machine-checked. [V] obligation stated but not discharged. [O] open problem. [F] withdrawn or expected to fail. [S] no implementation, simulation, or physical test exists.

A claim marked [P] is a mathematical argument written by hand. It is not a Lean proof, and it does not by itself establish that any compiled program satisfies it. The Lean statements that would mechanise [P] results are given in Appendix G; they are [V] until discharged.

