Appendix B — Fixed-Point Arithmetic

B.1 Concretisation

For scale s \in \mathbb{N} and integer x \in \mathbb{Z}, define the exact concretisation

\gamma_s(x) = \left[\, x 2^{-s} - 2^{-s-1},\; x 2^{-s} + 2^{-s-1} \,\right],

i.e. the set of reals that round to x under round-to-nearest. The family \{\gamma_s(x)\}_{x \in \mathbb{Z}} is a partition of \mathbb{R} into half-open intervals of width 2^{-s}. Setting h := 2^{-s-1},

\gamma_s(x) = \left[ x2^{-s} - h,\; x2^{-s} + h \right], \qquad |\gamma_s(x)| = 2h = 2^{-s}.

Define also the enlarged concretisation

\gamma_s^{\oplus}(x) = \left[ x2^{-s} - 2h,\; x2^{-s} + 2h \right],

which is the concretisation of x widened by one ulp on each side.

B.2 Proposition (the naive addition condition is false) [P]

The condition

\gamma_s(x + y) \supseteq \gamma_s(x) + \gamma_s(y)

fails for every s and for infinitely many (x,y).

Proof. Take s = 1, so h = 1/4. Take x = y = 1. Then

\gamma_1(1) = [1/2 - 1/4,\; 1/2 + 1/4] = [1/4,\; 3/4],

\gamma_1(1) + \gamma_1(1) = [1/2,\; 3/2].

But

\gamma_1(2) = [1 - 1/4,\; 1 + 1/4] = [3/4,\; 5/4].

The interval [1/2, 3/2] is not contained in [3/4, 5/4], since 1/2 < 3/4 and 3/2 > 5/4. The failure is structural: the sum of two intervals of width 2h has width 4h, while the concretisation of any integer has width 2h. ∎

Consequence. The v0.3 soundness condition is withdrawn [F]. The corrected condition is B.3.

B.3 Lemma (outward-rounded addition) [P]

For all x, y \in \mathbb{Z} for which x + y does not saturate,

\gamma_s^{\oplus}(x + y) \;\supseteq\; \gamma_s(x) + \gamma_s(y).

Proof. Let a = x2^{-s} + \delta, b = y2^{-s} + \delta' with |\delta|, |\delta'| \le h. Then

a + b = (x+y)2^{-s} + (\delta + \delta'), \qquad |\delta + \delta'| \le 2h.

Hence a + b \in \gamma_s^{\oplus}(x+y) = [(x+y)2^{-s} - 2h, (x+y)2^{-s} + 2h]. ∎

Remark. The price of soundness is one guard ulp. Equivalently, one may compute at scale s+1 and round the result to scale s; the lemma then holds with \gamma_s in place of \gamma_s^{\oplus}. The monitor's registry declares which convention is used, and the Lean development must match it exactly.

B.4 Lemma (multiplication error generator) [P]

Let a = x2^{-s} + \delta, b = y2^{-s} + \delta', |\delta|, |\delta'| \le h. Then

ab \;\in\; xy\,2^{-2s} \;+\; \left[\,-e(x,y),\; +e(x,y)\,\right],
\qquad
e(x,y) = h\left(|x| + |y|\right)2^{-s} + h^{2}.

Proof. Expand:

ab = xy\,2^{-2s} + x2^{-s}\delta' + y2^{-s}\delta + \delta\delta'.

Bound each term: |x2^{-s}\delta'| \le |x|2^{-s}h, |y2^{-s}\delta| \le |y|2^{-s}h, |\delta\delta'| \le h^2. Triangle inequality gives the stated e. ∎

Consequence. The product is representable exactly at scale 2s with centre xy, plus an error generator of half-width e(x,y). It is not representable at scale s with bounded relative error: e grows linearly in \max(|x|,|y|). This is the reason the v0.3 condition failed and the reason the corrected condition requires an explicit error generator rather than a bare containment.

B.5 Lemma (saturation detection is sound) [P]

Let M be the maximum representable magnitude at the declared word width. Define \mathrm{sat}(z) = \mathrm{sign}(z)\cdot M when |z| > M, and z otherwise, with flag f = \mathbf{1}[|z| > M]. Then f = 1 whenever the true result exceeds the representable range, and when f = 1 the monitor rejects.

Proof. Direct from the definition of \mathrm{sat} and the comparison |z| > M. ∎

Remark. The lemma is trivial; its importance is architectural. Because saturated results are unbounded rays rather than intervals, no finite concretisation contains them. Soundness is therefore recovered not by widening, but by rejection. Any implementation that clamps silently violates B.5 and invalidates O-3.

B.6 Lemma (error accumulation under affine iteration) [P]

Let x^{+} = Ax + b with \|A\| = \alpha. Suppose one step of the fixed-point computation introduces a per-step error generator of half-width \eta. Then after k steps the accumulated error half-width is at most

E_k \;\le\; \eta \sum_{j=0}^{k-1} \alpha^{j}
\;=\; \begin{cases} \eta \dfrac{1 - \alpha^{k}}{1 - \alpha}, & \alpha \ne 1,\\[2mm] \eta k, & \alpha = 1.\end{cases}

Proof. By induction. For k=1, E_1 \le \eta. Assume E_k \le \eta\sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\alpha^j. One further step maps an error of half-width E_k through A, giving \alpha E_k, and adds \eta:

E_{k+1} \le \alpha E_k + \eta \le \eta \sum_{j=0}^{k-1}\alpha^{j+1} + \eta = \eta\sum_{j=0}^{k}\alpha^{j}. \qquad \blacksquare

Consequence for the declared plants. Plant 1's A has \alpha = 1 (double integrator: an eigenvalue on the unit circle). Hence E_k grows linearly in k, unboundedly. This is not a numerical curiosity; it is why the envelope must be intersected with X_t^{(0)} at every step (Appendix C.7) and why the propagation horizon must be finite and declared. A monitor that propagates without intersection accumulates rounding error without bound and will eventually trip the feasibility gate for reasons unrelated to the agent.

B.7 Arithmetic soundness theorem [P]

Theorem B.7. Let a computation be a finite composition of additions, multiplications by registered constants, and affine images, each implemented with outward-rounded operators B.3 and B.4 and with saturation detection B.5. If no saturation flag is set, the computed set contains the exact real image of the input set.

Proof. Induction over the computation DAG, using B.3 and B.4 at each node and B.6 for the affine iteration, with the flag check excluding the saturated case. ∎

Status. Theorem B.7 is a paper proof. Its Lean mechanisation is obligation O-3 [V]. The statement must be phrased over the compiled semantics, including flag propagation; a statement over idealised rationals would not cover the Rust code (Appendix G.2).

