Appendix C — Zonotope Algebra

C.1 Definitions

A zonotope with centre c \in \mathbb{R}^n and generator matrix G = [g_1, \dots, g_m] \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times m} is

Z(c,G) = \left\{\, c + \sum_{j=1}^{m} \beta_j g_j \;:\; \beta \in [-1,1]^m \,\right\} = c + G[-1,1]^m.

The number m is the order. The canonical form of Appendix B and §3.3 requires G to have no zero columns and columns sorted lexicographically.

C.2 Lemma (support function) [P]

For every k \in \mathbb{R}^n,

\sup_{z \in Z(c,G)} k^{\top} z \;=\; k^{\top} c + \|G^{\top} k\|_1.

Proof. By linearity,

\sup_{\|\beta\|_\infty \le 1} k^{\top}(c + G\beta) = k^{\top} c + \sup_{\|\beta\|_\infty \le 1} \sum_{j=1}^m \beta_j (k^{\top} g_j).

The supremum of a linear functional over the cube is attained at \beta_j = \mathrm{sign}(k^{\top} g_j), giving \sum_j |k^{\top} g_j| = \|G^{\top}k\|_1. ∎

C.3 Lemma (affine image) [P]

For x \mapsto Ax + b,

AZ(c,G) + b \;=\; Z(Ac + b,\; AG).

Proof. A(c + G\beta) + b = (Ac+b) + (AG)\beta, and \beta ranges over the same cube. ∎

C.4 Lemma (Minkowski sum of zonotopes) [P]

Z(c_1, G_1) + Z(c_2, G_2) = Z(c_1 + c_2,\; [\,G_1 \;\; G_2\,]).

Proof. Sums of the form c_1 + G_1\beta_1 + c_2 + G_2\beta_2 are exactly c_1+c_2 + [G_1\;G_2](\beta_1,\beta_2) with (\beta_1,\beta_2) ranging over the cube of order m_1+m_2. ∎

C.5 Proposition (one-step successor of the linear plant) [P]

For x^{+} = Ax + Bu + w with w \in W = w_c + \mathrm{diag}(w_r)[-1,1]^n, the exact successor of Z(c,G) under a held input u is

F\big(Z(c,G), u, W\big) = Z\!\left(Ac + Bu + w_c,\; \left[\,AG \;\; \mathrm{diag}(w_r)\,\right]\right).

Proof. Combine C.3 and C.4 with the interval representation of W. ∎

Remark. On Plant 1 the successor is therefore available in closed form. The certificate in Mode L is not offloading computation; it binds the invocation. This is stated in §7.1 and is the reason Plant 2 is required to test the certificate's non-redundant role.

C.6 Proposition (exact containment in a polytope) [P]

Let P = \bigcap_{i=1}^{\ell}\{x : k_i^{\top} x \le b_i\} and let Z = Z(c,G) be compact and convex. Then

Z \subseteq P \iff \forall i \in \{1,\dots,\ell\}:\; k_i^{\top} c + \|G^{\top}k_i\|_1 \le b_i.

Proof. ( \Leftarrow ) If the inequality holds for all i, then by C.2, \sup_{z\in Z} k_i^{\top} z \le b_i, so every z \in Z satisfies every k_i^{\top}z \le b_i, i.e. z \in P. ( \Rightarrow ) If Z \subseteq P then for each i, \sup_{z \in Z} k_i^{\top} z \le b_i because the supremum is attained (compactness) at some z^{\ast} \in Z \subseteq P. ∎

Correction to the v0.4 note. The support-function test is a linear sufficient condition for zonotope-in-zonotope containment (Appendix C.6b) but is exact for zonotope-in-polytope containment, because a polytope is defined by its supporting halfspaces. Since K is a polytope, the check in §7.1 is exact, not merely conservative. The conservatism enters elsewhere: in the zonotope over-approximation of the true reachable set (C.7, C.8) and in the polytope over-approximation of K, if any. This distinction must be preserved in the Lean statements: it is the difference between an obligation that is discharged by C.6 and one that is not.

C.6b Proposition (zonotope-in-zonotope containment is hard) [O]

Deciding Z(c_1, G_1) \subseteq Z(c_2, G_2) is co-NP-complete in general, with exact polynomial algorithms only when the dimension n or the generator count m_2 is fixed. A linear sufficient condition is obtained by replacing the target zonotope with its H-representation over-approximation and applying C.6, or by the linear-programme formulation for AH-polytope containment, whose size grows linearly in the number of target hyperplanes.

Status. [O]. This proposition is stated to bound what can be claimed: whenever the monitor checks containment in a zonotope rather than a polytope, the check is conservative and its conservatism is part of the accounting of §4.4.

C.7 Proposition (sound over-approximating intersection) [P]

Let a constrained zonotope be

Z_c = \left\{\, c + G\beta \;:\; \beta \in [-1,1]^m,\; A_c \beta = b_c \,\right\},

and let a strip be S = \{x : |y - Hx| \le \epsilon\}. Then:

1. The intersection Z_c \cap S is a constrained zonotope.
2. Discarding the equality constraints of a constrained zonotope yields a zonotope that contains it:
   Z(c,G) \supseteq Z_c.

Proof. (2) The zonotope is the same set with the constraints A_c\beta = b_c removed; removing constraints can only enlarge a set. (1) is the closure property of the constrained-zonotope class under intersection with strips and halfspaces: intersecting with |y - Hx| \le \epsilon adds two affine constraints in (x,\beta), which become two equality constraints in the lifted variable after the standard slack construction. ∎

Corollary (the operator \cap^{\sim}). Define

Z \cap^{\sim} S \;:=\; \mathrm{Proj}\big( (Z \text{ lifted to } Z_c) \cap S \big) \text{ re-expressed as a zonotope by discarding constraints.}

By C.7, Z \cap^{\sim} S \supseteq Z \cap S. The operator is sound and computable; its conservatism equals the volume shed by the discarded constraints, which is reported in the slack accounting of §4.4.

Status. [P]. The operator named in §4.2 is thus specified and proved sound, not merely asserted to exist. Its implementation in fixed point is O-2 [V]; the fixed-point version must be shown to contain the exact-real version, which composes B.7 with C.7.

C.8 Proposition (sound order reduction) [P]

Let Z = Z(c,G) with G = [g_1,\dots,g_m], m > m'. Partition the columns into a kept block G_K and a discarded block G_D = [g_{m'+1},\dots,g_m]. Then

Z(c,G) \;\subseteq\; Z\!\left(c,\; \left[\,G_K \;\; \mathrm{diag}\!\left(\textstyle\sum_{j \in D} |g_j|\right)\right]\right),

where the sum is taken entry-wise (row-wise absolute values).

Proof. For any \beta,

c + \sum_{j} \beta_j g_j = c + \sum_{j \in K}\beta_j g_j + \sum_{j \in D}\beta_j g_j,
\qquad \left| \sum_{j \in D}\beta_j g_j \right| \le \sum_{j\in D}|g_j| \text{ entry-wise.}

The right-hand term therefore lies in the axis-aligned box \prod_i [-\sum_{j\in D}|g_{ij}|, +\sum_{j\in D}|g_{ij}|], which is exactly the zonotope generated by \mathrm{diag}(\sum_{j\in D}|g_j|) with \beta \in [-1,1]^n. ∎

Added width. The reduction increases the i-th coordinate's generator-mass by 2\sum_{j\in D}|g_{ij}|. This quantity, and not a Hausdorff distance, is what the monitor records as slack. The choice of which columns to keep is free; keeping the m' columns of largest Euclidean norm minimises the added coordinate mass in a least-squares sense but is not optimal in general. The optimal column selection is a subset-selection problem; the monitor uses the greedy rule and reports the resulting slack.

C.9 Theorem (reachability soundness under intersection and reduction) [P]

Theorem C.9. Suppose the true state satisfies x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t, and let

\mathcal{X}_{t+\Delta} \;=\; \mathrm{Red}_{m}\!\left( \mathrm{Reach}(\mathcal{X}_t, a_{\mathrm{held}}, W, \Delta) \cap^{\sim} X_{t+\Delta}^{(0)} \right)

with \cap^{\sim} as in C.7 and \mathrm{Red}_m as in C.8. Then x_{t+\Delta} \in \mathcal{X}_{t+\Delta}, provided \mathrm{Reach} is sound for the held control and the disturbance model.

Proof. The true successor lies in \mathrm{Reach}(\mathcal{X}_t, a_{\mathrm{held}}, W, \Delta) by the definition of \mathrm{Reach} and the antecedent x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t. It lies in X_{t+\Delta}^{(0)} because the observation at t+\Delta is consistent with the true state by the definition of the measurement-consistent set, assuming the error bound \epsilon(y_{t+\Delta}) is valid. Hence it lies in the intersection, which is contained in the over-approximation \cap^{\sim} (C.7), which is contained in the reduced zonotope (C.8). ∎

Remark. Theorem C.9 is the envelope-soundness obligation O-2. The paper proof depends on three side conditions that must each be checked: \mathrm{Reach} soundness for a_{\mathrm{held}}, validity of the error bound, and the fixed-point version of the operators. The first is plant-specific; the second is a provenance requirement; the third composes B.7 with C.7 and C.8.

C.10 Corollary (the intersection is not optional) [P]

Without the intersection with X_{t+\Delta}^{(0)}, the generator count and the coordinate masses are non-decreasing in t, since Reach appends n disturbance generators per step (C.4) and order reduction only adds mass. Hence by B.6 with \alpha = 1 the envelope's interval-hull widths grow without bound along every trajectory, and the feasibility gate of Appendix F trips after a finite number of steps independent of the agent's behaviour.

Proof. Immediate from C.4, C.8 and B.6. ∎

Consequence. TC-12 of §14 is not a diagnostic; it is a demonstration of a theorem. A monitor without intersection is provably unusable on Plant 1, not merely empirically worse.

