Appendix D — Measurement-Robust Barrier Certificates

D.1 Discrete-time barrier condition

Let h : X \to \mathbb{R} be a barrier function whose sublevel set \mathcal{H} = \{x : h(x) \ge 0\} is a subset of S_{\mathrm{safe}}. The nominal (non-robust) discrete-time condition for a controller k is

h\big(F(x, k(x), w)\big) \;\ge\; (1-\eta)\, h(x) \qquad \forall w \in W,

for a decay rate \eta \in (0,1], or the stronger h(F(\cdot)) \ge 0 when no decay is required.

D.2 Lipschitz data

The registry supplies constants L_h, L_f, L_{\mathcal{L}_f h} \ge 0 such that, for all x, x' in the declared domain,

|h(x) - h(x')| \le L_h \|x - x'\|,

\|f(x) - f(x')\| \le L_f \|x - x'\|,

\|\mathcal{L}_f h(x) - \mathcal{L}_f h(x')\| \le L_{\mathcal{L}_f h}\|x - x'\|,

where f(x) = F(x, k_B(x), w) for the worst-case w and \mathcal{L}_f h is the Lie derivative appearing in the continuous-time formulation of the MR-CBF construction. The constants come from the trusted registry and sensor provenance, never from the agent's certificate (§7.2). Supplying them from the certificate would let the agent choose the constants that make its own proposal pass, which defeats the monitor.

D.3 Lemma (measurement-robust tightening) [P]

Let \hat{x} = x + e with \|e\| \le \epsilon. Suppose the nominal condition holds at the estimate,

h\big(F(\hat{x}, k_B(\hat{x}), w)\big) \;\ge\; (1-\eta) h(\hat{x}).

Then the condition at the true state holds with margin

h\big(F(x, k_B(\hat{x}), w)\big) \;\ge\; (1-\eta) h(x) \;-\; \mu,
\qquad
\mu \;=\; \Big( L_{\mathcal{L}_f h} + L_h \,\|k_B\| \Big)\,\epsilon \;+\; L_h (1 + \eta)\,\epsilon.

Proof. Write the difference

h(F(x, k_B(\hat{x}), w)) - (1-\eta)h(x)
= \underbrace{\left[ h(F(\hat{x}, k_B(\hat{x}), w)) - (1-\eta)h(\hat{x}) \right]}_{\ge 0 \text{ by hypothesis}}
+ \underbrace{\left[ h(F(x,\cdot,w)) - h(F(\hat{x},\cdot,w)) \right]}_{\text{term (I)}}
+ \underbrace{(1-\eta)\left[ h(\hat{x}) - h(x) \right]}_{\text{term (II)}}.

Term (I) is bounded below by -L_h \|F(x,\cdot,w) - F(\hat{x},\cdot,w)\|. The map F(\cdot, k_B(\hat{x}), w) differs between x and \hat{x} through the dynamics and through the controller's dependence on its argument; bounding the former by L_{\mathcal{L}_f h}\epsilon (the Lie-derivative Lipschitz term) and the latter by L_h\|k_B\|\epsilon gives (I) \ge -(L_{\mathcal{L}_f h} + L_h\|k_B\|)\epsilon. Term (II) is bounded below by -(1-\eta)L_h\epsilon \ge -L_h\epsilon. Summing gives the stated \mu, with the (1+\eta) grouping absorbing the two L_h contributions. ∎

Remark. The exact grouping of constants depends on whether the controller is evaluated at \hat{x} or at x, and on whether \eta multiplies the estimate or the true value. The registry declares which variant is used; the Lean statement and the Rust checker must use the same variant. A mismatch here is the most likely source of an unsound Mode N check, and Appendix G.4 makes the matching a CI condition.

D.4 Linearisation remainder

When the condition is checked on a linearisation \bar{f} of f at \hat{x}, the remainder must be bounded. For f twice differentiable with Hessian bounded by M on the declared domain,

\| f(x) - \bar{f}(x) \| \;\le\; \tfrac{M}{2}\,\|x - \hat{x}\|^2 \;\le\; \tfrac{M}{2}\,\epsilon^2.

The remainder is quadratic in \epsilon, hence dominated by the linear \mu of D.3 for small \epsilon. It is nevertheless included in the tightened bound, because the monitor must not rely on an asymptotic argument. M is registry data.

D.5 Farkas semantics, corrected

The v0.3 draft stated that the Farkas witness proves "the linearised constraints under the tightened bound are feasible." This is withdrawn [F].

Correct statement. Let the tightened constraint set be a system of linear inequalities C x \le d. Farkas' lemma states that the system is infeasible if and only if there exists \lambda \ge 0 with \lambda^{\top} C = 0 and \lambda^{\top} d < 0. Equivalently, Cx \le d implies a^{\top}x \le \beta if and only if (a,\beta) lies in the cone generated by the rows of (C,d) together with the trivial nonnegativity generators. The certificate witness is a vector \lambda exhibiting one of these implications.

The monitor checks the implication, not satisfiability: it verifies that the tightened constraint is implied by the linearised barrier condition and the registered Lipschitz data, or that the negation is infeasible. The distinction matters because a feasible system of constraints says nothing about whether the action satisfies the barrier; an implication does.

D.6 What Mode N does not establish

Mode N establishes: given the registry's Lipschitz constants, the registry's error bound, the registry's linearisation remainder, and a valid Farkas witness, the tightened barrier inequality holds at the true state. It does not establish:

· that the registry's constants are correct for the plant (they are TCB items, Appendix I.5);
· that the barrier function's sublevel set is a subset of S_{\mathrm{safe}} (this is a separate inclusion to be checked);
· that the nominal condition held at the estimate (this is what the certificate asserts, and the monitor must recompute it rather than trust the assertion);
· that the plant matches the model.

These are [V] or [O] items in the ledger of Appendix K.

