Appendix E — Viability, the Backup Set, and the End-to-End Theorem

E.1 Backup set

Fix a backup controller k_B and a horizon T. Define the backup set at error bound \epsilon as

\mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) = \left\{\, x \in S_{\mathrm{safe}} \;:\; \forall e, \|e\| \le \epsilon,\; \forall w_0,\dots,w_{T-1} \in W:\; x_j(x, e, w_{0:j-1}) \in S_{\mathrm{safe}} \;\forall j \le T \,\right\},

where x_0 = x and x_{j+1} = F(x_j, k_B(x_j + e_j), w_j).

E.2 Proposition (under-approximation) [P]

If \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) as defined in E.1 is non-empty and the terminal condition x_T \in S_{\mathrm{safe}} is strengthened to x_T \in \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon), then \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) is a subset of the maximal viability kernel of S_{\mathrm{safe}} under k_B with the same error and disturbance bounds.

Proof. By definition, every trajectory from a point in \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) remains in S_{\mathrm{safe}} for all horizons (by the recursive strengthening), which is the defining property of membership in the viability kernel. Hence \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) \subseteq \mathrm{Viab}(S_{\mathrm{safe}}). ∎

Conservatism. The monitor may reject actions whose successors lie in the true viability kernel but outside \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon). The gap is reported (Appendix F). The converse inclusion does not hold in general, and the specification does not claim it.

E.3 Lemma (\epsilon_{\max} is well defined and monotone) [P]

The map \epsilon \mapsto \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) is non-increasing in the inclusion order: \epsilon_1 \le \epsilon_2 \Rightarrow \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_1) \supseteq \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_2).

Proof. The defining condition of \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_2) quantifies over a strictly larger set of admissible error sequences e_j than does \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_1). Hence any point satisfying the stronger condition satisfies the weaker one. ∎

Corollary. The budget

\epsilon_{\max}(x) = \sup\{\epsilon : \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) \text{ is invariant under } k_B \text{ and } \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) \subseteq S_{\mathrm{safe}}\}

is well defined whenever the set is non-empty, and is computable by bisection: test invariance at a candidate \epsilon, halve or double, terminate when the bracket width falls below the declared resolution. The resolution is a registry parameter and its effect on conservatism is reported.

Fixed-point caveat. In the fixed-point implementation the computed backup set \tilde{K} must be an under-approximation of \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_{\max}), because a computed set that is too large would make the invariance obligation easier than it should be. The computation therefore uses inward rounding for the set construction and outward rounding for the invariance check. This asymmetry is deliberate and must be preserved in the Lean statement; a symmetric rounding convention would invalidate O-5 in one direction or the other.

E.4 Obligation 0, strong form [V]

\forall x \in \tilde{K},\; \forall e : \|e\| \le \epsilon_{\max},\; \forall w \in W:\; F(x, k_B(x+e), w) \in \tilde{K},

where \tilde{K} is the fixed-point-computed backup set and F is evaluated with outward-rounded arithmetic. The obligation quantifies over every error below the budget, not merely at the budget, because the runtime error at a given step may be smaller than \epsilon_{\max} and the invariance must hold there too. This is the correction of §5.2 and it is strictly stronger than the v0.3 statement.

E.5 Theorem (end-to-end confinement), proof skeleton [V]

Theorem E.5. Assume O-2 (envelope soundness, Theorem C.9), O-3 (arithmetic soundness, Theorem B.7), O-5 (Obligation 0, E.4), and O-7 (authorization soundness, Appendix G.3). Let the true state satisfy x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t. If the monitor accepts a_t, then for every admitted w \in W, the true successor satisfies x_{t+1} \in \tilde{K}, and by induction x_{t+k} \in S_{\mathrm{safe}} for all k \ge 0 along the backup trajectory.

Proof skeleton.

Base. x_t \in \mathcal{X}_t by hypothesis.

Step. The acceptance condition (§6) includes F(\mathcal{X}_t, a_t, W) \subseteq \tilde{K}. By O-2, the true successor lies in the computed successor set, hence in \tilde{K}.

Delay. The state is augmented with the held-control sequence (§2), so the transition used in the step is over x^{\ast}_t = (x_t, a_{t-1},\dots,a_{t-\Delta}). The induction is over x^{\ast}, not over x. Without augmentation the step fails, because the successor depends on controls that are not components of x_t.

Induction. Suppose x^{\ast}_{t+k} \in \tilde{K} \times A^{\Delta}. The monitor either accepts a new action (re-entering the step) or the backup controller is in force. In the latter case O-5 gives x^{\ast}_{t+k+1} \in \tilde{K}. In the former case the step applies again. Since \tilde{K} \subseteq S_{\mathrm{safe}} by construction (E.1–E.2), membership in \tilde{K} implies membership in S_{\mathrm{safe}}. ∎

Status. The skeleton is conditional on O-5. It is not a theorem. Specifically:

· The step relies on O-2 holding for the augmented state, which requires the envelope to be propagated over the augmented dynamics. This is stated in §2 and §4 but is not proved.
· The induction presumes that the monitor's decision sequence does not admit a step where neither a new acceptance nor the backup is in force. The architecture must exclude that case by construction, and the exclusion is an obligation (Appendix G.5), not an automatic consequence.
· The paper proof says nothing about the compiled monitor; O-7 is required for that.

E.6 Expected failure on Plant 2 [F]

For the pendulum with bounded delay and measurement error, the backup set \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon_{\max}) is expected to be empty or to fail E.4, because the linearised backup controller's region of attraction shrinks below the measurement-error uncertainty when \epsilon_{\max} is set conservatively enough to cover the sensor's stated accuracy. If E.4 fails for Plant 2:

· Theorem E.5 is false for Plant 2.
· The end-to-end claim is withdrawn for Plant 2, per §17 and O-5.
· The Mode N certificate remains specified but is not attached to a confinement claim; it is attached only to the per-step barrier condition of Appendix D, which is a weaker statement.
· The monograph records the withdrawal in the register of Appendix J.

The specification is written so that this outcome is a result, not a defect: a falsifiable architecture that predicts its own failure on a declared plant has discharged part of its obligation by doing so.

