Appendix F — The Feasibility Gate

F.1 Per-constraint margins

For K = \bigcap_{i=1}^{\ell}\{x : k_i^{\top}x \le b_i\} and envelope \mathcal{X}_t = Z(c,G),

\mu_i(\mathcal{X}_t) \;=\; b_i - \left( k_i^{\top} c + \|G^{\top} k_i\|_1 \right), \qquad i = 1,\dots,\ell.

By C.6, \mu_i \ge 0 for all i if and only if \mathcal{X}_t \subseteq K. The margins are therefore not a heuristic; they are the exact containment test.

F.2 Proposition (the gate is sound and complete for the containment question) [P]

\mathcal{X}_t \subseteq K \iff \min_i \mu_i(\mathcal{X}_t) \ge 0.

Proof. Immediate from C.6. ∎

Status. The gate answers does this envelope satisfy this constraint? It is exact. It does not answer is the measurement quality sufficient? That is the separate budget question of F.3.

F.3 Why volume fails [P]

Claim. Volume is not a sufficient statistic for containment: two envelopes of equal volume may have different containment status in K, and volume is monotone under inclusion (so the v0.3 statement that it is not monotone is withdrawn [F]).

Proof of insufficiency. Take K = [-1,1]^2. Let

Z_1 = Z\!\left(\binom{0}{0},\; \mathrm{diag}(1,1)\right), \qquad \mathrm{vol}(Z_1) = 4,

Z_2 = Z\!\left(\binom{0}{0.95},\; \mathrm{diag}(5,\,0.1)\right), \qquad \mathrm{vol}(Z_2) = 10 \cdot 0.2 = 2.

Z_1 \subseteq K. Z_2 extends to x \in [-5,5], hence Z_2 \not\subseteq K. The contained envelope has larger volume than the non-contained one. Hence volume does not order containment status. Moreover \mathrm{vol} is monotone under inclusion (A \subseteq B \Rightarrow \mathrm{vol}(A) \le \mathrm{vol}(B)), so the v0.3 rejection reason is factually wrong even though the v0.3 conclusion (do not gate on volume) is correct. ∎

F.4 Why the hull-width product fails [P]

The width product w(Z) = \prod_i 2\sum_j|G_{ij}| is the volume of the axis-aligned bounding box, and it is also a sufficient-statistic failure.

Proof. Same K = [-1,1]^2. Take

Z_3 = Z\!\left(\binom{0}{0},\; \mathrm{diag}(1,1)\right), \qquad w(Z_3) = 4,

Z_4 = Z\!\left(\binom{0}{0.95},\; \mathrm{diag}(5,\,0.05)\right), \qquad w(Z_4) = 10 \cdot 0.1 = 1.

Z_3 \subseteq K, Z_4 \not\subseteq K, and w(Z_4) < w(Z_3). The width product fails for the same reason volume fails: it collapses a shape into a scalar. ∎

Consequence. The v0.3 replacement of volume by width product is also withdrawn [F]. The correct gate is per-constraint (F.1–F.2), with the scalar budget \epsilon_{\max} as a separate, prior test.

F.5 Combined gate

The runtime gate is the conjunction of two independent tests:

1. Budget test. If \|\epsilon(y_t)\| > \epsilon_{\max}(\hat{x}_t), enter mitigation. This is the measurement quality test; it does not look at the envelope.
2. Containment test. If \min_i \mu_i(\mathcal{X}_t) < 0, reject the action. This is the shape test; it does not look at the measurement quality beyond what is already encoded in \mathcal{X}_t.

The two tests answer different questions (Appendix §5.3) and neither implies the other. A monitor that implements only the budget test accepts envelopes that violate K; a monitor that implements only the containment test attempts to certify envelopes whose construction was invalid.

