Appendix H — Policy Revision Calculus

H.1 R1 conditions

A restrictive refinement from S_k to S_{k+1} must satisfy

\text{(i)}\quad S_{k+1} \subseteq S_k,

\text{(ii)}\quad w(S_{k+1}) \ge \beta\, w(S_{\mathrm{baseline}}), \qquad \beta \in (0,1],

\text{(iii)}\quad \forall x \in \partial S_{k+1},\; \exists \tau \text{ of length } T_{\mathrm{avail}} \text{ with } \tau(0) = x,\; \tau(j) \in S_{k+1} \;\forall j.

Condition (i) is the refinement; (ii) is the baseline-relative availability floor; (iii) is trajectory preservation, which prevents the refinement from leaving a non-empty set of dead states.

H.2 Proposition (the per-revision ratio compounds) [P]

Let w(S_{k+1}) \ge \alpha\, w(S_k) for each revision and w(S_0) = w(S_{\mathrm{baseline}}). Then after n revisions,

w(S_n) \;\ge\; \alpha^n \, w(S_{\mathrm{baseline}}).

For \alpha = 0.95 and n = 14, \alpha^n = 0.95^{14} \approx 0.488.

Proof. Immediate by induction: w(S_n) \ge \alpha w(S_{n-1}) \ge \alpha^n w(S_0). The numerical value follows from \ln 0.95 \approx -0.0513 and 14 \times 0.0513 \approx 0.718, so e^{-0.718} \approx 0.488. ∎

Consequence. The v0.3 condition with S_{\min} and a per-revision ratio is withdrawn [F]. A per-revision ratio permits an attacker who can submit a sequence of individually-approved R1 revisions to erode the operating envelope by a factor of two in fourteen steps. The baseline condition (ii) bounds the total erosion by \beta, independent of the number of revisions.

H.3 Proposition (baseline condition does not compound) [P]

Under condition (ii) alone, w(S_k) \ge \beta\, w(S_{\mathrm{baseline}}) for every k, by definition. The bound does not depend on k.

Proof. Trivial. ∎

Remark. The baseline condition is weaker per revision than a per-revision ratio with \alpha > \beta: a single large restriction is permitted as long as it stays above the floor. This is deliberate. The floor is the governance decision; the per-revision ratio was an accident of formulation that made the governance decision depend on the number of revisions rather than on their content.

H.4 Rollback lemma [P]

Let the policy store be an append-only list (p_0, p_1, \dots, p_n) with strictly increasing identifiers and a hash chain H_k = \mathrm{hash}(p_k \,\|\, H_{k-1}). Then for any certificate referencing p_j, a monitor that holds H_n and the head identifier n can decide whether j \le n and whether H_j matches the stored chain, and hence reject any rollback to j < n that has not been explicitly authorised.

Proof. The comparison j \le n is decidable from the two integers; the hash comparison is decidable from the stored chain and the certificate's p_hash field. Rejection at Layer 1 follows before the proof checker runs. ∎

H.5 R2 and R3

R2 (model correction). The replacement model must discharge O-2 and O-3 for the corrected dynamics, and every obligation that depended on the old model must be re-discharged. The obligation set is not inherited; a corrected model is a new model with its own ledger.

R3 (governance or domain extension). The change requires an assurance-case delta identifying new hazards introduced by the expansion, a new model-validity assessment, and governance authorisation. The delta is attached to the policy version as a digest; the digest's presence and approval status are preconditions for PolicyMatch. The monitor does not evaluate the delta's content; it evaluates the presence of an approved digest. The evaluation of content is a governance function, and its trustworthiness is a TCB assumption (Appendix G.6, row "backup set" is analogous: certification is external, use is internal).

