Appendix I — Falsification Protocol, Formalised

I.1 Formal statements

Let \mathcal{T} be the test corpus, N the monitor, P the plant model.

F1. \exists\, (x, a, w, \pi) \in \mathcal{T} such that N accepts \pi, x \in \mathcal{X}(\pi), and F(x, a, w) \notin \tilde{K}.

F2. \exists\, (\pi, \pi') \in \mathcal{T} with q(\pi') \le q(\pi) and N accepts both; or p_{\mathrm{id}}(\pi') < p_{\mathrm{id}}(\pi) and N accepts \pi'.

F3. Let \mathrm{HWM} be the measured high-water mark of the decision path on the target and B_{\mathrm{static}} the static bound. F3 fires if \mathrm{HWM} > B_{\mathrm{static}}.

F4. \exists\, (x, a, w) \in \mathcal{T} with A_{\mathrm{safe}}(\mathcal{X}(x)) \neq \emptyset and F(x, a, w) \notin \tilde{K} for the fallback actually selected.

F5. \frac{|\{\pi \in \mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{nom}} : N \text{ rejects } \pi\}|}{|\mathcal{T}_{\mathrm{nom}}|} > 0.05.

F6. \mathrm{median}_{t}\big(\sigma_t^{(i)}\big) > 3.0 for some direction i with bounded X_t^{(0)} in that direction.

F7. \exists\, \pi \in \mathcal{T} with \|\epsilon(y)\| > \epsilon_{\max}(\hat{x}) and N accepts \pi.

I.2 Search completeness

A falsification criterion is only as strong as the search that failed to violate it. The protocol therefore requires:

· the corpus generator to be declared (fuzzer, adversarial search, exhaustive enumeration over a finite abstraction);
· the coverage to be reported against the declared input domain (byte-level coverage for the parser, state-space coverage for the plant-level criteria);
· the negative result to be reported as "no violation found under the declared search", never as "no violation exists".

I.3 Why a p99 cannot falsify a WCET claim [P]

Claim. An observed p99 latency \ell_{99} is consistent with an unbounded worst-case execution time.

Proof. Latency is a function of the input and the platform state. A p99 is the 99th percentile of the empirical distribution over the observed inputs. If the input domain contains a path not exercised by the corpus, the p99 places no constraint on that path's cost. Hence \ell_{99} \le B does not imply \sup_{\mathrm{inputs}} \ell \le B. A measured high-water mark is a valid lower bound on the true worst case, and the static bound is an upper bound on the modelled worst case; the two together are the correct instrument, and the p99 is not. ∎

Consequence. F3 is stated against \mathrm{HWM} and B_{\mathrm{static}}, as in §15.

I.4 Model-mismatch campaign

The campaign perturbs the plant outside W and measures the monitor's response. It makes no safety claim. Its purpose is to document the boundary of the model's validity: the point at which the monitor's rejections stop being attributable to the agent and start being attributable to the model. The campaign's output is a degradation curve, not a pass/fail.

I.5 Pre-registration discipline

The criteria F1–F7 and their thresholds are fixed before simulation. A threshold adjusted after seeing data is a new criterion and must be declared as such, with the adjustment recorded. This is the difference between a pre-registered protocol and a narrative.

