Appendix L — Open Problems

L.1 Exact fixed-point \cap^{\sim}. Appendix C.7 proves soundness of constrained-zonotope intersection followed by constraint discarding. The conservatism shed by discarding is not bounded in the paper proof. A bound would tighten the slack accounting of §4.4 and reduce false rejections on Plant 2.

L.2 Sharp \mu. The tightening constant of D.3 is correct but not sharp; the grouping (1+\eta) over-approximates the term (II) contribution for small \eta. A sharper constant would enlarge \mathcal{C}_B(\epsilon) and delay the expected failure of E.6. Whether a sharp enough constant exists to make E.6 succeed on the pendulum is an open question, and it is the specific question on which the Plant 2 claim turns.

L.3 Column selection in order reduction. C.8 proves containment for any partition of the generator columns. Optimal selection is combinatorial. A near-optimal greedy rule with a proved approximation ratio would reduce slack without weakening soundness.

L.4 Decision-path totality under lease failure. O-11 is architectural. The case in which the lease expires during the actuation commit is the hardest; a formal statement of the actuator's atomicity requirement is needed and is not given here.

L.5 Model validity. No part of this monograph establishes that the model matches the plant. The model-mismatch campaign documents the boundary. Closing the gap requires a separate theory of model validation, which is outside the scope of a runtime monitor.

L.6 Refinement of the arithmetic model. The Lean model and the Rust code share integer semantics by construction, but the target platform's arithmetic is a further refinement that is not addressed. If the platform's integer operations deviate from the declared two's-complement saturating semantics, B.5 does not apply and O-3 is not discharged for the compiled program.

